    {
      "turn": 4,
      "doctor_question": "{\"intent\": \"Assess for catatonic features such as catalepsy, waxy flexibility, or feeling physically frozen/unable to move.\", \"question\": \"When you are standing there with your hands raised",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Oh, you mean like... am I stuck? No, I don't feel frozen or anything like I can't move. I could put my hands down whenever I want to, honestly. They just get kind of tired and heavy after a few minutes. \n\nKind of like when I was helping my buddy move last weekend. He lives in this awful third-floor walk-up apartment, and the stairwell is incredibly narrow. We were trying to pivot this giant couch around the corner—it was this hideous crushed mustard yellow velvet. I don't even know where he got it, probably at that thrift shop on 5th Street. You know the one? It always smells faintly like mothballs and old soup. I bought a desk lamp there once, but the wiring was completely shot and it blew the fuse for half my apartment. The electrician who came out to fix it spent the whole time complaining about his son's little league baseball team. He said the kids don't even care about the game, they just want the post-game snacks. Which I totally get, because my favorite part of playing sports as a kid was always the orange slices at halftime. They were always perfectly cold in those little plastic baggies. \n\nBut yeah, I'm not physically trapped or frozen when I stand by the copier. I just kind of do it.",
